\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A sub-Riemannian structure consists of a smooth bracket-generating distribution $D\subset TM$ and a smooth inner product on $D$. A horizontal (admissible) curve has $\dot\gamma(t)\in D_{\gamma(t)}$ almost everywhere, and length $L(\gamma)=\int\|\dot\gamma(t)\|_D\,dt$. The distance is the infimum of lengths of horizontal connections. Let $\gamma_0,\gamma_1:[0,1]\to M$ be smooth horizontal paths with $\gamma_0(0)=\gamma_1(0)=q_0$ and linearly independent initial velocities, expressed as $\dot\gamma_0(0)\wedge\dot\gamma_1(0)\ne0$. The corner connection from $\gamma_0(1)$ to $\gamma_1(1)$ reverses $\gamma_0$ and then follows $\gamma_1$; its length is $L(\gamma_0)+L(\gamma_1)$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Does there always exist a horizontal path from $\gamma_0(1)$ to $\gamma_1(1)$ of length strictly less than $L(\gamma_0)+L(\gamma_1)$? \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1304.2590}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Can every horizontal corner formed by independent tangent directions be replaced by a shorter horizontal connection?

